From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] accel/rocket: sequence the completion poll against scheduler
 teardown

rocket_job_fini() cancels the poll timer and its work and then calls
drm_sched_fini().  In that order the cancel does not hold: a job still in
flight retires through the poll, rocket_job_handle_irq() re-arms the next
task, and rocket_job_hw_submit() starts the timer again behind the cancel
that has already run.  What it fires into is a scheduler drm_sched_fini()
is in the middle of tearing down, and a fence signalled from there belongs
to a job that no longer has an owner.

The two operations are not interchangeable and both are needed, so
sequence them: stop the poll ACTING before the scheduler goes, and CANCEL
it after, at a point where nothing can re-arm it.  A poll_dying flag does
the first half -- the work item checks it and returns, which is enough,
since the timer only ever queues that work.

The flag is not a one-way latch.  struct rocket_core is allocated for the
device, not for a binding, so it outlives an unbind whenever another core
keeps the device alive; a core that came back would otherwise carry the
dying flag from its previous teardown and never retire a job again.
rocket_job_init() clears it.  On a single-core RK3576 every unbind is the
last one and the array is reallocated, which is why the ROCK 4D and the
H96 both miss this.

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h |  8 ++++++++
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c  | 28 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 2 files changed, 34 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h
index 4779aa3a7..535e0dce4 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h
@@ -123,6 +123,14 @@ struct rocket_core {
 	 */
 	unsigned int poll_seq;
 	unsigned int poll_work_seq;
+	/*
+	 * Set while the core is being torn down, to keep the poll from starting
+	 * hardware work the scheduler is no longer there to own.  Not a one-way
+	 * latch: struct rocket_core outlives an unbind whenever another core
+	 * stays bound, so rocket_job_init() clears it and a rebinding core polls
+	 * again.
+	 */
+	bool poll_dying;
 
 	struct drm_gpu_scheduler sched;
 	u64 fence_context;
diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
index 66faaf4ba..0e98f042a 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
@@ -714,6 +714,10 @@ static void rocket_poll_work_fn(struct work_struct *work)
 {
 	struct rocket_core *core = container_of(work, struct rocket_core, poll_work);
 
+	/* The core is going away; rocket_job_fini() owns what is left. */
+	if (READ_ONCE(core->poll_dying))
+		return;
+
 	/*
 	 * The submit this work was queued for has already been retired -- by the
 	 * DMA-error interrupt thread, or by a reset.  Whatever is in flight now
@@ -940,6 +944,13 @@ int rocket_job_init(struct rocket_core *core)
 	hrtimer_setup(&core->poll_timer, rocket_poll_timer_fn, CLOCK_MONOTONIC,
 		      HRTIMER_MODE_REL);
 	atomic_set(&core->poll_active, 0);
+	/*
+	 * Cleared, not merely initialised: this core may be one rocket_job_fini()
+	 * has already set, since struct rocket_core survives an unbind whenever
+	 * another core keeps the device around.  Leaving it set would give the
+	 * rebound core a poll that never retires anything.
+	 */
+	WRITE_ONCE(core->poll_dying, false);
 	spin_lock_init(&core->fence_lock);
 	mutex_init(&core->job_lock);
 
@@ -981,12 +992,25 @@ int rocket_job_init(struct rocket_core *core)
 
 void rocket_job_fini(struct rocket_core *core)
 {
+	/*
+	 * The poll starts hardware work -- rocket_job_handle_irq() re-arms a
+	 * multi-task job's next task -- and drm_sched_fini() does not wait for a
+	 * work item already queued, so the two have to be sequenced rather than
+	 * merely both done.
+	 *
+	 * Stop the poll ACTING first, and CANCEL it after the scheduler is gone.
+	 * Cancelling first is the ordering that does not work: a job still in
+	 * flight re-arms the timer from hw_submit() behind the cancel, and it
+	 * then fires into a scheduler that drm_sched_fini() has torn down.
+	 */
+	WRITE_ONCE(core->poll_dying, true);
+
+	drm_sched_fini(&core->sched);
+
 	atomic_set(&core->poll_active, 0);
 	hrtimer_cancel(&core->poll_timer);
 	cancel_work_sync(&core->poll_work);
 
-	drm_sched_fini(&core->sched);
-
 	cancel_work_sync(&core->reset.work);
 	destroy_workqueue(core->reset.wq);
 }
